## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical cost-benefit analysis thinking for privacy-conscious mobile features with offline-first architecture. It tests understanding of storage trade-offs, energy budgeting, consent management, and long-term maintenance considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first storage and local geofence processing to ensure reliability without network connectivity
- Energy budgeting for continuous location monitoring and CPU usage for geofence checks
- Privacy, consent, and data minimization requirements for location-based features
- Cloud synchronization trade-offs for shared calendar data and conflict resolution
- Rollout planning with phased deployment and offline fallback behavior
- Technical debt and drift/maintenance costs over the feature lifecycle

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: register geofence, trigger locally, and schedule reminder
registerGeofence(reminder.id, reminder.coordinates, reminder.radius, async (trigger) => {
  const localReminder = await db.getReminder(reminder.id);
  if (localReminder) {
    await notificationManager.show(localReminder);
    await syncQueue.add({ reminderId: reminder.id, triggeredAt: Date.now() });
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a lightweight edge optimization with clear cost/benefit trade-offs, focusing on data movement, energy, and privacy in mobile environments.

## Key Concepts

- Content-based fingerprinting
- Bloom filters for on-device dedup
- Bandwidth vs CPU energy trade-offs
- Privacy considerations and optional cloud verification

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: compute hash and check Bloom filter
function canSend(record, bloom) {
  const h = hash(record); // deterministic fingerprint
  if (bloom.mightContain(h)) return false;
  bloom.add(h);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate Bloom filter false positive rate given device memory constraints?
- How would you rollout to mitigate drift in data patterns and ensure continued value?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's ability to translate product goals into a financially grounded technical plan, balancing on-device ML costs, user privacy, and rollout risk.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML cost modeling (energy, memory, inference latency)
- Data transfer savings and network impact
- Privacy controls and regulatory compliance
- OTA update cadence and maintenance costs
- Rollout strategy, drift monitoring, and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-cost model for CBA calculations
function cba(params) {
  const energy = params.inferences * params.energyPerInference;
  const memory = params.modelSize + params.cacheSize;
  const dataSavings = params.uploadSize * params.reductionRate;
  return { energy, memory, dataSavings };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to quantify costs and benefits of a client-side policy engine with offline operation, privacy constraints, and staged rollout.

## Key Concepts

- On-device budgeting and telemetry
- Offline-first synchronization
- Privacy, consent, and compliance
- Rollout strategies and risk controls
- Break-even horizon and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: compute CBA estimate
function computeCBA(inputs){
  const ramMB = inputs.ram || 30;
  const energyPerHour = inputs.energy || 1.0;
  const dataTxKB = inputs.dataTx || 150;
  return { ramMB, energyPerHour, dataTxKB };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust the CBA if privacy laws tighten data retention or encryption requirements?
- What telemetry would you deploy to validate drift and performance post-rollout, and how would you act on anomalies?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests candidate ability to design a mixed on-device/offload chat triage, evaluating lifecycle costs and business impact of privacy-preserving UX.

## Key Concepts

- On-device NLP model size and latency
- Cloud handoff economics
- Local storage and OTA updates
- Compliance and user trust
- Rollout and measurement plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for on-device classifier trigger
function triage(input) {
  const intent = onDeviceModel.predict(input);
  if (isComplex(intent)) {
    return cloudHandoff(input);
  }
  return generateResponse(intent);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure incremental savings from reduced cloud fetches?
- What telemetry would you collect to monitor quality without compromising privacy?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to reason about on-device ML, privacy, and cost trade-offs for a common feature.

## Key Concepts
- On-device ML footprint
- Energy and storage budgeting
- Privacy by design and opt-in telemetry
- Cadence and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Outline: computeDelivery(decisionContext) using a tiny local model
function shouldDeliver(notif, context, model) {
  // placeholder for feature: compute score and threshold
  const score = model.predict([notif.priority, context.timeOfDay, context.usage]);
  return score > 0.5;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you estimate energy impact in production?
- What telemetry would you collect if user opt-ins?

## Why This Is Asked
Privacy-preserving on-device experiments enable rapid iteration without raw data leaving devices, aligning with regulatory scrutiny and low-latency user experience goals.

## Key Concepts
- On-device flag evaluation and cohort management
- Local storage and memory budgeting
- Delta-based telemetry with minimal data exposure
- Safe rollout, rollback, and veto mechanisms
- Compliance and auditability considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Local flag evaluation snippet (pseudo)
function evalFlag(user, flagRepo) {
  const cohort = user.cohortId;
  const flag = flagRepo.getFlag(cohort);
  return flag.evaluate(user);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes on-device ML, multi-language NLP, privacy, and business trade-offs in a realistic, consumer-facing feature.

## Key Concepts

- On-device inference and model sizes
- Multilingual NLP and drift handling
- Update strategies and telemetry design
- Privacy/compliance and data localization
- Cost-to-benefit modeling and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-evaluation snippet for latency/energy estimates
function estimatePostModeration(postBytes, modelSizeMB) {
  const latencyMs = 0.004 * postBytes + 0.5 * modelSizeMB;
  const energyJ = latencyMs * 0.01;
  return { latencyMs, energyJ };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure model drift and trigger retraining on-device?
- How would you handle unsafe content in languages with low availability data?
